import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Ui/Demo
import ../../../Demos/Lobby

meta = {
	title: "Live and channels",
	description: "Live features on the Roc Solid server platform: channels that push typed, access-checked messages to pages over one stream per browser tab, with hooks for what pages say and who comes and goes; and the lower-level rooms (who is here) and topics (messages between calls) they are built on.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "channels", label: "Channels: live data for pages" },
		{ id: "channel-hooks", label: "What pages say, and hooks" },
		{ id: "channel-stream", label: "One stream per tab" },
		{ id: "rooms", label: "Rooms: who is here" },
		{ id: "topics", label: "Topics: messages between calls" },
		{ id: "which", label: "Which to use" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · live and channels"
				title="Live updates, from the server to the page"
			>
				Some pages must change while someone looks at them: a chat, a board others edit, a count of who
				is online. For those, the server pushes messages to the page. A <strong>channel</strong> is the
				usual way: a typed stream of messages, checked against who may read it, that pages subscribe to.
				Channels are built on two simpler parts, <strong>rooms</strong> and <strong>topics</strong>, which
				a stream of events can use directly. All of it lives in the server's memory: what must survive a
				restart belongs to an <a href={Paths.platform_actors}>actor</a> or a
				<a href={Paths.platform_database}>table</a>.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="channels">Channels: live data for pages</Heading>
			<p>
				A channel is defined in a route's <code>+server.roc</code>, beside its endpoints, so it has a path
				like any route. Its definition is <code>channel!</code>, a check: given the request, the path's
				params and what the route's <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a> handed on, it
				answers who the subscriber is (<code>Channel.allow(who)</code>), or a refusal. Its type says what the
				server publishes on it, and what pages may say on it. This page's lobby lets everyone in:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.channel_lobby.file} source={Sources.channel_lobby.rocx} />
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo waiting="Join, here and in another tab.">
			<div data-demo="lobby"><Lobby /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> puts each channel in <code>Generated/Api</code> as a function of its params,
				as it does for endpoints: <code>Api.channel_platform_live_lobby({"{}"})</code> here, or
				<code>Api.channel_org_org_news({"{ org }"})</code> for a channel at <code>/org/[org]/news</code>. It
				is typed with the <code>Channel(who, message, said)</code> that <code>channel!</code>'s annotation
				gives. <code>Generated/Api</code> imports no route file, so a job, an actor or any other server module
				can import it and publish on a channel by name, type-checked.
			</p>
			<p>
				A component subscribes from an <a href={"${Paths.components}#effects"}>effect</a>. The subscription
				ends when the effect runs again or the component goes away:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.demo_lobby.file}, in part"} source={Sources.demo_lobby.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>A subscriber hears these events, in order, one at a time:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Resync</code></strong>: the page may have missed something, so it should read its
					data again (<code>refresh!</code>). It comes each time the tab's stream connects or reconnects,
					when the server sees the subscriber fell behind, and when a page asks for one
					(<code>Endpoint.resync!</code>). The route's <code>on_open!</code> messages come right after it.
				</li>
				<li><strong><code>Message(message)</code></strong>: something published, as the channel's type.</li>
				<li><strong><code>Refused(status, why)</code></strong>: the check refused the subscriber as the stream opened.</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Ended(why)</code></strong>: the check ran again, on one of the stream's regular
					beats (every 15 s), and refused it. So someone who loses access stops hearing within a beat.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The server publishes with
				<code>Endpoint.publish!(Api.channel_org_org_news({"{ org: \"acme\" }"}), news)</code>, from a request,
				a job or an actor. Only the subscribers with the same params hear it, in the order it was published.
				A page's first data, as the server renders it, comes from a <code>get!</code> endpoint on the same
				path, read with <code>Endpoint.get!</code>; the channel carries what changes after that.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Only the latest matters?</strong> <code>Endpoint.publish_latest!(channel, key, message)</code>
					is for values where only the newest counts (a cursor's position). A subscriber that is behind
					gets only the newest message for each key, and these aren't ordered with the channel's other
					messages.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Falling behind.</strong> A subscriber that falls more than 256 messages behind hears
					<code>Resync</code> instead of what it missed, never a silent gap.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="channel-hooks">What pages say, and hooks</Heading>
			<p>
				Beside <code>channel!</code>, a route may define three hooks. Each is given the path's params, who
				the check said the subscriber is, the subscriber's tab id, and the channel itself (to publish on):
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>receive!</code></strong> handles what a page says
					(<code>Endpoint.say!(channel, said)</code>), after the same middleware and check, or refuses it.
					What a page says within one frame goes in one request.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>on_open!</code></strong> returns the messages a subscriber is sent first, each time
					it subscribes (who is here, say), right after its <code>Resync</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>on_close!</code></strong> is told when a subscription ends, and why:
					<code>Dropped</code> (the tab stays, but the page no longer subscribes: the component went, or
					follows another channel now), <code>Gone</code> (the tab closed or reloaded, its network went, or
					the server stopped), or <code>Refused(why)</code> (the check, at a beat). It runs outside the
					middleware, since losing access may be what ended it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<code>who</code> stays on the server, kept as JSON from the check until <code>on_close!</code>. A
				tab's id is random and kept for the tab's life (a reload keeps it); it names a browser tab, not a
				person.
			</p>
			<p>
				A tab's hooks run in order. If a page drops a channel and subscribes again, the route hears the end
				before the new start. When a new stream comes for a tab (after a reload or a reconnect) before the
				server has noticed the old one end, the old one ends first: its <code>on_close!</code> calls run
				before the new stream's checks and <code>on_open!</code>. So a route can keep "who is here" per
				tab: join on <code>on_open!</code>, leave on <code>on_close!</code> (at once when
				<code>Dropped</code>; after a moment when <code>Gone</code>, in case the tab comes back), with
				nothing to count by hand. The <a href={Paths.ecosystem_presence}>presence package</a> does this for you, with names, colours and cursors.
			</p>

			<Heading id="channel-stream">One stream per tab</Heading>
			<p>
				All of a tab's subscriptions share one server-sent events stream,
				<code>GET /rocx/live?tab=…&amp;c=/org/acme/news&amp;c=…</code>, however many there are, and two
				components on one channel share one subscription. The browser's runtime opens the stream once the
				page has rendered. If it drops, the runtime opens it again after a wait that doubles each time,
				from half a second up to 30 s.
			</p>
			<p>
				The stream sends its id first. When what the page subscribes to changes (once the render that
				changed it is done), the runtime posts the change, naming that id, and the stream stays open. With
				nothing left to subscribe to, it says so, then closes the stream. A static copy of a site (only
				files, no server) has no channels: a subscriber hears <code>Refused(404, …)</code>, once.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>On the stream</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>event: hello</code>, <code>data: </code> the stream's id</td><td>first: the id the runtime names the stream by when it changes the tab's subscriptions (192 random bits, this stream's alone)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: resync</code>, <code>data: /org/acme/news</code></td><td>read your data again; <code>on_open!</code>'s messages follow</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: message</code>, the URL, then <code>data: {"{\"title\":…}"}</code></td><td>what was published, as JSON</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: refused</code>, the URL, then <code>data: {"{\"status\":403,\"why\":\"Forbidden\"}"}</code></td><td>the check refused this channel; the stream goes on for the others</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: ended</code>, the URL, then why, as a JSON string</td><td>the check refused it at a beat</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>: beat</code></td><td>every 15 s (<code>--live-beat-ms</code>): the server is there, and has checked each channel again</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: replaced</code></td><td>a newer stream for this tab took its place, and this one ends. A page still reading it is another tab with the same id (a duplicated tab copies it), and takes an id of its own</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>No call is held open.</strong> The host keeps the open streams and passes each channel's
					messages on. Roc runs only briefly, one call at a time: a check, a hook, a beat's checks. Compare
					an endpoint's <code>events!</code> stream, which is a call for as long as it is open (paused while
					it waits, so it holds no thread, but it keeps its stack), and one per page rather than one per
					tab.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Middleware applies.</strong> Each check is a request the host makes of the app, for the
					channel's own path, with the tab's cookies, behind the app's middleware and the route's, just as a
					<code>GET</code> of that path would be. The cookies are the stream's, or those of the last post
					that changed its subscriptions, so a page that signs in without a reload reaches members'
					channels. An app's sign-in guard guards a channel by its path; <code>/rocx/live</code> itself never
					reaches the app's middleware.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A lost tab is gone in about 35 s.</strong> A tab whose network went never closes its
					stream. The next beat's write goes unacknowledged, and 20 s later the server drops the connection
					and tells <code>on_close!</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What pages say</strong> goes in <code>POST /rocx/live/send?tab=…&amp;stream=…</code>: a JSON
					list of <code>{"{\"channel\":\"/org/acme/news\",\"message\":…}"}</code>, with the stream's changes
					(<code>{"{\"channel\":…,\"subscribe\":true}"}</code>, <code>{"{\"channel\":…,\"unsubscribe\":true}"}</code>).
					It is answered 204, or 200 with each refusal
					(<code>{"{\"refused\":[{\"index\":0,\"status\":403,\"why\":…}]}"}</code>). A post from another
					site's page is refused (403), and so is a change naming no open stream of the tab (404).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Limits:</strong> a stream carries at most 64 channels; a post carries at most 256 items
					and 1 MiB.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="rooms">Rooms: who is here</Heading>
			<p>
				Rooms and topics are the building blocks under channels, for a stream of events you write yourself
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#streams"}>streams</a>). A room counts who is in it. A call joins one
				with <code>Live.join!</code> and stays in it until the call ends: for a stream, until its client goes
				or it crashes. <code>Live.count!</code> says how many are in a room, from anywhere. This stream sends
				the count when it starts, and again whenever someone comes or goes:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_online.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_online.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Live.next!(ms)</code> waits for what the call hears: <code>Heard</code> (a message on a topic
				it listens to, or someone joining or leaving a room it is in), <code>Quiet</code> once
				<code>ms</code> milliseconds pass with nothing, or <code>Gone</code> when the client has gone. So a
				loop like this one sleeps until there is something to say, and ends with its client.
			</p>

			<Heading id="topics">Topics: messages between calls</Heading>
			<p>
				A topic carries messages to every call listening to it. <code>Live.listen!</code> starts listening,
				for as long as the call lasts. <code>Live.publish!</code>, from any request, job or actor, sends a
				message to everyone listening:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_shouts.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_shouts.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A component listens to such a stream as to any endpoint's events
				(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>events</a>). On the server, that is a call that stays open (paused
				while it waits, so it holds no thread), and in the browser it uses one of the six connections a
				browser opens to a host over HTTP/1.1, for as long as it is open. For what pages follow live, a
				channel is better.
			</p>

			<Heading id="which">Which to use</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>For</th><th>Use</th><th>Lasts</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>live data for pages, checked</td><td>a channel (<code>channel!</code>, <code>Endpoint.subscribe!</code>, <code>Endpoint.publish!</code>)</td><td>while the subscribers last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>who is here, now</td><td>a room (<code>Live.join!</code>, <code>Live.count!</code>), or a channel's hooks</td><td>while the calls in it last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>telling every listening call</td><td>a topic (<code>Live.publish!</code>, <code>Live.listen!</code>)</td><td>while the listeners last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a count, a cache, a token used once</td><td>a value in memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}><code>Kv</code></a>: <code>Kv.increment!</code>, <code>Kv.set!</code>, <code>Kv.take!</code>)</td><td>its time to live, or until a restart</td></tr>
						<tr><td>state many requests change, one at a time</td><td>an actor (<a href={Paths.platform_actors}><code>Actor</code></a>: <code>ask!</code>, <code>tell!</code>)</td><td>in the database: survives restarts</td></tr>
						<tr><td>data, queried every way</td><td>a table (<a href={Paths.platform_database}><code>Db</code></a>)</td><td>in the database</td></tr>
						<tr><td>work that may fail and should run again</td><td>a job (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}><code>Job.enqueue!</code></a>)</td><td>in the database, until done</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The platform's tests open streams that join a room and count each other, publish to listeners,
					open one stream for many channels (one refused), say things on one, end one whose check fails at
					a beat, fall behind on one, and change what a stream carries by its id (a dropped channel is told
					<code>Dropped</code>, the stream's end <code>Gone</code>, in order; a post from another site, or
					with another tab's id, refused). They keep 40 streams open on two Roc threads while requests still
					answer. In a browser, two components share one stream, a change reaches the stream without a
					reconnect, and a dropped stream reconnects a little later each time.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
